[
  {
    "id": "ex42-tab",
    "type": "tab",
    "label": "SITL 42 PX4 reposition CHANGE_MODE",
    "disabled": false,
    "info": "Throwaway probe. The fourth PX4 cell: mid-AUTO_TAKEOFF with CHANGE_MODE set. Identical to 40 in every respect but the flag.",
    "env": []
  },
  {
    "id": "ex42-comment",
    "type": "comment",
    "z": "ex42-tab",
    "name": "PX4 reposition mid-AUTO_TAKEOFF with CHANGE_MODE set",
    "info": "Flow: identical to 40 — Set MIS_TAKEOFF_ALT=40 -> Arm -> Takeoff -> 6 s -> Move reposition, mid-climb. The ONLY difference is changeMode TRUE.\n\nWHY THIS RUN EXISTS: example 30 is confounded and cannot carry the claim it appears to carry. 30 sent the flag SET, but it also waited 8 s after a takeoff to the 2.5 m default, which completes in about 2 s — so PX4 had already settled into AUTO_LOITER before the reposition went out. AUTO_LOITER on its own satisfies the no-flag branch. So 30 ACCEPTED is explained equally well by the flag or by the mode, and nothing in it isolates CHANGE_MODE on PX4.\n\n40 supplies the other half: flag clear, mid-AUTO_TAKEOFF, MAV_RESULT_DENIED (2), measured. This run flips only the flag against that same known-DENIED state. If it accepts, CHANGE_MODE alone is sufficient on PX4 and the rule holds. If it also denies, PX4 needs the eligible mode regardless of the flag, and 30 passed purely on AUTO_LOITER.\n\nSOURCE HYPOTHESIS (PX4 Navigator::run): applies the setpoint if ((uint32_t)cmd.param2 & 1) != 0 OR nav_state == NAVIGATION_STATE_AUTO_LOITER, and only while ARMING_STATE_ARMED. The flag satisfies the first branch, so the prediction is ACCEPTED.\n\nWHAT THE RIG SHOULD RECORD:\n1. param status — did MIS_TAKEOFF_ALT=40 take? Without it the climb window is ~2 s and the reposition lands in AUTO_LOITER, which measures the wrong cell and would look like a clean PASS\n2. reposition status — result, detail, resultCode\n3. param2 on the wire must read 1, which is the proof the flag really was set\n4. Whether the vehicle broke off the climb toward the target"
  },
